WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
Linux security is the practice of operating Linux systems so required work can happen

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.220
while unnecessary risk is reduced.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.160
The goal is not to make a server impossible to use,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.220
or to turn every workstation into a locked box with no business value.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.640
The goal is reliable operation with controlled exposure:

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:22.420
the right users can do the right work, services run for a clear reason, software is

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.760
maintained, activity is visible, and recovery is possible when something goes wrong.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.660
Linux appears in many places.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.160
It may run a web server, a cloud instance, a container host, a developer workstation,

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:39.300
a network appliance, an embedded device, or a small

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.800
homelab system.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.860
Those environments have different risk levels, but the fundamentals are familiar.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.120
Security depends on installation choices, configuration, accounts, file permissions,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.860
packages, services, SSH access, firewall rules, logs, backups,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.000
and the way changes are approved and

00:00:58.220 --> 00:00:59.180
validated.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.560
Least privilege is a central theme.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.060
A user should not have more access than the role requires.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.480
A service should not run if nobody owns it or needs it.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.340
A port should not be reachable just because a package opened it.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.880
A file mode should match the purpose of the file, not convenience during setup.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.340
These decisions are small, but they compound into a security posture.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.160
Linux is powerful because administrators can change almost anything.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.620
That power requires discipline: inventory first, test changes,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.800
use a change window for important systems, document assumptions, keep rollback options,

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.260
monitor results, and maintain

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.520
backups.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.020
Secure operation is a lifecycle, not a single command, tool, or hardening checklist.
